---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RpeCalculator from '../components/RpeCalculator.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { rpeFraction } from '../lib/onerepmax.js';
import { webAppNode, webPageNode } from '../lib/schema';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
import { SITE, formatDate, reviewedFor } from '../config/site';

const title = 'RPE Calculator — Estimate 1RM from RPE & Reps in Reserve';
const description =
  'Estimate your one rep max from a set rated by RPE, then plan weights for any reps and RPE. Uses the reps-in-reserve scale and six published 1RM formulas.';
const path = '/rpe-calculator/';
const site = (Astro.site?.toString() ?? '').replace(/\/$/, '');
const url = `${site}${path}`;
// Reference chart built from the same function the calculator uses.
const chartRpes = [10, 9, 8, 7, 6];
const chartRows = Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => i + 1).map((reps) => ({
  reps,
  cells: chartRpes.map((rpe) => rpeFraction(reps, rpe)),
}));
const jsonLd = [
  webPageNode({ site, url, name: title, description, reviewed: reviewedFor(path) }),
  webAppNode({ site, url, name: '1RMHub RPE Calculator', description,
    features: ['1RM estimate from weight, reps and RPE', 'Weight for any reps at any RPE', 'Reps-in-reserve scale', 'kg and lb'] }),
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} crumbs={[{ name: 'RPE Calculator', href: path }]} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <h1>RPE Calculator</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Estimate your one rep max from a set you rated by RPE — no need to go to failure — then work out
    the weight for your next set at any reps and RPE.
  </p>
  <p class="page-meta">Last reviewed <time datetime={reviewedFor(path)}>{formatDate(reviewedFor(path))}</time> · <a href="/methodology/">How we calculate</a></p>

  <RpeCalculator />

  <div class="prose">
    <section aria-labelledby="what-rpe-heading">
      <h2 id="what-rpe-heading">What is RPE in lifting?</h2>
      <p class="answer">
        RPE (rating of perceived exertion) rates how hard a set was on a 1–10 scale. In strength
        training it is usually tied to <strong>reps in reserve</strong>: RPE 10 means you could not have
        done another rep, RPE 9 means one more was left, RPE 8 means two were left, and so on.
      </p>
      <p>
        This reps-in-reserve version of the scale was described by Zourdos and colleagues in 2016.
        It lets you train hard without going to failure every set, and it adjusts to how strong you
        are on the day.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="how-rpe-heading">
      <h2 id="how-rpe-heading">How the RPE calculator works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Reps to failure:</strong> reps done + reps in reserve. 5 reps at RPE 8 ≈ 7 reps to failure.</li>
        <li><strong>1RM estimate:</strong> the same six published formulas as the <a href="/">1RM calculator</a> run on those reps to failure, and their average is shown with the full range.</li>
        <li><strong>Next-set weight:</strong> the Epley equation solved backwards for your target reps plus reps in reserve.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Because reps to failure must stay within 12, sets that are both long and easy (for example
        10 reps at RPE 7) are not estimated — they are too far from a max to predict reliably.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="chart-heading">
      <h2 id="chart-heading">RPE chart: % of 1RM by reps and RPE</h2>
      <p>
        Each cell is the percentage of your 1RM for that many reps at that RPE, calculated with the
        Epley equation on reps to failure. Real lifters vary, so use it as a starting point.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Estimated percentage of 1RM by reps and RPE (Epley, reps to failure)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Reps</th>{chartRpes.map((r) => <th scope="col" class="num">RPE {r}</th>)}</tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {chartRows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.reps}</th>
                {row.cells.map((c) => <td class="num">{c === null ? '—' : `${(c * 100).toFixed(1)}%`}</td>)}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="disclaimer">— means more than 12 reps to failure, where estimates are not shown.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="accuracy-rpe-heading">
      <h2 id="accuracy-rpe-heading">How accurate is RPE?</h2>
      <p>
        RPE depends on your own judgement. A review of the research by Stronger by Science reports
        that, on average, lifters misjudge their reps in reserve by about one rep — enough to move a
        1RM estimate by a few percent. If you are new to RPE, check your ratings occasionally by
        taking a set to a safe failure (for example on a machine or with safeties set), and use the
        <a href="/">standard 1RM calculator</a> for sets taken close to failure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="rpe-faq-heading">
      <h2 id="rpe-faq-heading">RPE calculator: common questions</h2>
      <h3>What does 5 reps at RPE 8 mean?</h3>
      <p>
        You did 5 reps and could have done about 2 more, so roughly 7 reps to failure. For 100 kg × 5
        at RPE 8, the calculator estimates a 1RM of about 121.7 kg (range 117.5–124.0 kg).
      </p>
      <h3>Is RPE 10 the same as failure?</h3>
      <p>
        On the reps-in-reserve scale, RPE 10 means you could not have done another rep with good form —
        no reps left. It does not mean grinding out a rep with broken technique.
      </p>
      <h3>Can I use half RPEs like 8.5?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. The reps-in-reserve scale includes half steps, and so does this calculator: RPE 8.5 means
        you might have managed 1 or 2 more reps, so it counts as 1.5 reps in reserve.
      </p>
      <h3>Is training by RPE better than percentages?</h3>
      <p>
        Both work. In an 8-week study of trained men, the group that chose weights by RPE and the group
        that used fixed percentages both gained strength in the squat and bench press, with a small
        advantage for RPE that was not statistically significant (Helms et al., 2018). Many programs
        combine the two.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <AdSlot />

  <RelatedLinks items={[LINKS.rpeGuide, LINKS.home, LINKS.percent, LINKS.trainingMax]} />
  <SourceList ids={['zourdos', 'sbsrir', 'helms2018', 'epley']} />
</BaseLayout>
